+//! The cap as a value: how many series a store tracks, and what it says about one that does not fit.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`, and the half of it that counts nothing. A cap is a
+//! *policy* — a number somebody chose, and a rule for the two answers it can give — so it lives
+//! here as data rather than inside the registry that applies it: a deployment's number can be
+//! argued about, tested and changed without reading the code that keeps the ledger, and a test can
+//! ask what the rule says without a store anywhere near it.
+//!
+//! # What the limit counts
+//!
+//! **Series the store tracks**, and not frames, not records and not bytes. The distinction is the
+//! whole of this step: a client that sends a million samples of `cpu,host=h0` is a client this
+//! store is happy to serve — that is one series, a million records — and a client that sends one
+//! sample each of a million `host=` values is the one that costs money, because every one of those
+//! names is a dictionary entry, an index vocabulary term and a part of an answer's identity. A cap
+//! on *requests* would refuse the first client; a cap on *series* refuses the second, which is the
+//! client whose data the store cannot afford (`docs/cardinality.md` measures what a series costs).
+//!
+//! # The rule, in two sentences
+//!
+//! **A series the store already tracks is always admitted**: a cap must not break a series that is
+//! being reported, or a deployment that reached its limit would start losing the data it already
+//! holds, one sample at a time, with no error anywhere. **A series it has never seen is admitted
+//! while the ledger has room for it and refused once it does not** — the refusal is
+//! [`Admission::Rejected`], it carries both numbers so that a log line or a client can say exactly
+//! what happened, and it is *counted* by the caller (`Registry::series_rejected`), because a policy
+//! nobody can see is a policy nobody can operate.
+
+use std::fmt;
+
+/// How many series a store tracks before it starts refusing ones it has never seen.
+///
+/// The number is a deployment's, not a law of the format, and it is the one number in this step
+/// that has to be defended rather than derived: see [`Cap::DEFAULT`] for the one this build ships
+/// and `docs/cardinality.md` for the arithmetic that makes it a budget rather than a guess.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Cap {
+    /// How many series fit.
+    limit: usize,
+}
+
+impl Cap {
+    /// The cap this build ships: **1024 series**.
+    ///
+    /// Chosen, not measured, and the page says so: it is the number of series a store of this
+    /// course's size can hold at the price `docs/cardinality.md` measures per series, rounded to
+    /// something a person can hold in their head and argue about in a review. It is deliberately
+    /// not a limit anybody hits by accident — every workload this course's own harnesses run stays
+    /// far inside it — so that the first refusal a deployment sees is one somebody set on purpose,
+    /// in `series.limit`, with the arithmetic in front of them.
+    pub const DEFAULT: Cap = Cap::new(1024);
+
+    /// A cap of `limit` series.
+    ///
+    /// Zero is a legal cap and means what it says: a store that tracks nothing may still hold what
+    /// it already has (a tracked series is always admitted), and may take no new series at all.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(limit: usize) -> Self {
+        Self { limit }
+    }
+
+    /// How many series this cap allows.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn limit(&self) -> usize {
+        self.limit
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Cap {
+    /// The policy as a deployment reads it back, for the line that prints it.
+    fn fmt(&self, _formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        todo!("the cap as a phrase, for the line that prints a deployment's policy")
+    }
+}
+
+/// What a cap says about one offered series.
+///
+/// Two answers and not three, because the third question a client might ask — *why not?* — is the
+/// one [`Admission::Rejected`] already answers by carrying the two numbers the decision was made
+/// from: how many series the store tracks, and what its cap allows. A reader of a log line or a
+/// client's error has both, and neither of them is a string somebody has to parse.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum Admission {
+    /// The store takes it: either it is already tracked, or there was room for one more.
+    Admitted,
+    /// The store refuses it: `tracked` series are in the ledger and the cap allows `limit`.
+    ///
+    /// The refusal is about a series **the store has never seen**. A series it already tracks is
+    /// admitted however full the ledger is, which is why this variant cannot mean "the store is
+    /// full" in the abstract: it means "there is no room for a name nobody has reported yet".
+    Rejected {
+        /// How many series the store was tracking when it decided.
+        tracked: usize,
+        /// How many its cap allows.
+        limit: usize,
+    },
+}
+
+impl Admission {
+    /// `true` when the store took the series.
+    ///
+    /// The accessor exists because both answers are values a caller branches on: a log line, a
+    /// counter and the ingest path's decision are all written against this and not against a
+    /// sentence somebody has to read.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn is_admitted(&self) -> bool {
+        matches!(self, Self::Admitted)
+    }
+}
+
+impl fmt::Display for Admission {
+    /// The decision as a reader gets it, naming the two numbers it was made from.
+    ///
+    /// Both answers are printable: a refused frame's log line is where an operator meets this, and
+    /// a taken one is a line a caller writes too.
+    fn fmt(&self, _formatter: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
+        todo!("the decision as a log line or a client could read it")
+    }
+}
