@@ -0,0 +1,347 @@
+//! The store's ledger of series: which ones it has admitted, what each one costs, and the cap that
+//! decides whether a name nobody has reported yet fits.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.18-cardinality-cost`. A series is a metric and a tag set — `cpu,host=h0,dc=east`
+//! — and everything in this store is keyed by one: a segment lays its columns out by series name, a
+//! query names one in `FROM … WHERE …`, and the sidecar index's vocabulary is built from their
+//! names (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`). What nothing before this step bounded is **how many of
+//! them there are**, and that number is the one an operator cannot see until it is expensive: a
+//! label whose value set the *application* chooses — `host=<pod name>`, `user=<id>`,
+//! `request_id=<uuid>` — turns one metric into a million series, and a store that holds a million
+//! series holds a million dictionaries, a million index vocabularies and a million names in every
+//! answer's identity. [`Cap`] is the policy that bounds it, and this module is the ledger that
+//! makes the cap a decision about a *store* rather than about one connection.
+//!
+//! # What the ledger counts
+//!
+//! The series this store has **admitted**, with what each one cost so far. Two numbers are enough
+//! to decide about a new name, and each has a trap in it:
+//!
+//! * [`Registry::tracked_count`] is the **live set** — how many names the ledger holds right now —
+//!   and it is what the cap's limit is measured against. Counting *records* instead would cap the
+//!   traffic rather than the cardinality, and a store with one series and a billion samples would
+//!   be refused while a store with a million one-sample series was not, which is backwards;
+//! * [`Registry::cost_of`] is what one series has cost: records, bytes, and the first and last
+//!   timestamp it was seen at. It is the number `docs/cardinality.md` does its arithmetic with,
+//!   and the number `lakeletq inspect series` prints.
+//!
+//! # Where the ledger comes from: the store, not a file of its own
+//!
+//! The ledger is **derived state**, and that is the rule it shares with the sidecar index
+//! (`lakelet/4.14-index-pruning`): a store cannot be wrong about its own cardinality because a file
+//! this module writes is missing or stale. [`Registry::of_store`] rebuilds it from what the store
+//! itself has — the records its log carries, which is every record the write path ever accepted
+//! (`docs/soak.md`: this build never truncates the log), pruned by the sidecar indexes of the
+//! segments it still holds. Opening a store is what does that, so a deployment that restarts does
+//! not start counting from zero and admit a fresh `limit` of new names on every restart — which
+//! would be a cap that leaks exactly as fast as the process manager restarts it.
+//!
+//! The prune is where the index earns its place here, and its direction is 4.14's:
+//!
+//! * a series **some** live index may hold is one the store still holds, and stays;
+//! * a series **no** live index may hold is one the store has forgotten — retention expired the
+//!   segments that carried it, a merge dropped them — and it leaves the ledger, which is what
+//!   makes room for a new name. A cap that counted names for ever would be a lifetime quota on a
+//!   store whose retention window deliberately forgets;
+//! * an index that is **missing or damaged** is [`SegmentIndex::unstated`], which may hold
+//!   everything. It therefore proves nothing and drops nothing: the ledger keeps a name it cannot
+//!   rule out, which costs tracking *precision* — the store counts a series it may no longer hold —
+//!   and never an answer, because an index is a hint and the store's records are the store.
+//!
+//! # The conservation identity
+//!
+//! Every offer is counted, exactly once, on one side of the cap:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! offered() == series_admitted() + series_rejected()
+//! ```
+//!
+//! and a series the cap refused is **not** in the ledger. That is the line a case can falsify, and
+//! it is the line a leak crosses: a refused name that is inserted anyway leaves `tracked_count`
+//! above the limit and the two counters short of `offered`. Nothing else in this module can be
+//! wrong about the cap without crossing it.
+//!
+//! A frame is judged as the batch it is, and the identity is over *series*: a frame whose records
+//! name three series is three offers, and a frame the cap refuses is a whole frame the store does
+//! not write — the write path stores a payload whole or not at all
+//! (`lakelet/4.5-batch-writes`), so a refusal that let half of a frame through would be an
+//! unstated partial write. Each series of a refused frame counts as rejected, including one the
+//! store already tracks, because what the counter answers is "did this store take this data".
+//!
+//! # What is yours
+//!
+//! The types and the signatures are the contract this step's spec, its ingest path and the tool are
+//! written against; the bodies are this step's work. The accessors and the conservation identity
+//! are given, because they are arithmetic. The three decisions that are not:
+//!
+//! * **where the ledger comes from.** It is *derived state*, and `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` is the
+//!   step that argued why: a store that is wrong about its own cardinality because a sidecar went
+//!   stale is the failure that rule exists to prevent. [`Registry::of_store`] rebuilds it from what
+//!   the store itself has, and the case that decides whether the cap is a policy or a decoration is
+//!   the **restart** — a ledger that counted only what this process admitted lets a fresh limit of
+//!   names in every time the process manager restarts the daemon;
+//! * **what a rebuild may prune**, and which direction of it is the safe one;
+//! * **what one payload costs each series it names.** Whatever rule you pick, the numbers have to
+//!   add up to the bytes the store was handed, or the arithmetic on the page is a claim.
+//!
+//! # What this module deliberately does not do
+//!
+//! * **it does not read a clock.** `now` is handed in by the caller, so a case can charge a series
+//!   at any timestamp it likes and two runs of one workload agree;
+//! * **it does not decide what a client sees.** A refusal is a value ([`Admission`]) and the count
+//!   of it; the service's ingest path is what turns it into a log line and a frame that is not
+//!   stored, and `docs/cardinality.md` says what the client can observe;
+//! * **it does not evict.** A cap that dropped the oldest series to make room would answer a query
+//!   with a hole, and no count of anything would say so. The line this store draws is: the series
+//!   it already holds are kept, and the ones it cannot afford are the ones it never takes.
+
+pub mod cap;
+
+pub use cap::{Admission, Cap};
+
+use std::collections::BTreeMap;
+use std::path::Path;
+
+use crate::engine::index::SegmentIndex;
+use crate::engine::query::series::Series;
+use crate::error::Error;
+
+/// What one series has cost this store so far.
+///
+/// Four numbers, and each of them is a question somebody asks about cardinality: how many records
+/// the series is (`records`), how many bytes they took (`bytes`), and when it was first and last
+/// seen (`first_millis`, `last_millis`, both inclusive, milliseconds since the Unix epoch). The
+/// pair of timestamps is what makes "a series that was reported once, six months ago" visible: it
+/// is a series the cap is holding room for and nothing is reporting any more, and deciding what to
+/// do about those is what the page argues and what a future step's eviction policy would need.
+///
+/// `bytes` is what the write path was handed for this series, shared out over the series a payload
+/// named by how many records each carried (see [`Registry::charge`]): the numbers add up to exactly
+/// the bytes this store accepted, which is what makes the total on the page arithmetic rather than
+/// a claim.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Cost {
+    /// Records charged to this series.
+    records: u64,
+    /// Bytes charged to this series.
+    bytes: u64,
+    /// The earliest timestamp it was charged at.
+    first_millis: u64,
+    /// The latest timestamp it was charged at.
+    last_millis: u64,
+}
+
+impl Cost {
+    /// How many records this series has cost.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn records(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.records
+    }
+
+    /// How many bytes this series has cost.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn bytes(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.bytes
+    }
+
+    /// The earliest timestamp this series was seen at, inclusive.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn first_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.first_millis
+    }
+
+    /// The latest timestamp it was seen at, inclusive.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn last_millis(&self) -> u64 {
+        self.last_millis
+    }
+}
+
+/// The series a store has admitted, what each one cost, and the counters a run is judged on.
+///
+/// A store holds one of these. It is not a cache and not an index: it is the answer to "how much
+/// cardinality does this store carry", which is the question the cap is a policy about, and it is
+/// built from the store's own records rather than kept beside them (`Registry::of_store`).
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Registry {
+    /// The policy: how many series fit.
+    cap: Cap,
+    /// The live set, by name, in name order — which is the order [`Registry::tracked`] answers and
+    /// therefore the order the tool prints one line per series in. Sorted rather than in arrival
+    /// order because a table that reorders itself between two runs is a table nobody can diff.
+    tracked: BTreeMap<String, Cost>,
+    /// Series offers this registry admitted.
+    admitted: u64,
+    /// Series offers it refused.
+    rejected: u64,
+}
+
+impl Registry {
+    /// An empty ledger under this cap.
+    ///
+    /// Empty is the honest start for a caller that has no store: a registry that invented series
+    /// would be a cap counting something nobody reported.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub const fn new(cap: Cap) -> Self {
+        Self {
+            cap,
+            tracked: BTreeMap::new(),
+            admitted: 0,
+            rejected: 0,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The ledger of what these payloads carry: every record they hold, charged to the series it
+    /// names.
+    ///
+    /// This is how a store's ledger is rebuilt from the records it kept, and it is **not** an
+    /// offer: nothing is judged by the cap and no counter moves, because these records are already
+    /// in the store. What the cap decides is what a client may *add* to it, and a store that
+    /// refused its own records would be a store whose cap depended on which way round it was
+    /// opened.
+    ///
+    /// A payload that is not a batch this build can read contributes nothing: the write path
+    /// accepted bytes, and bytes that name no series are not the ledger's business.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn of_payloads(cap: Cap, payloads: &[Vec<u8>]) -> Self {
+        let _ = (cap, payloads);
+        todo!("the ledger of the records these payloads hold")
+    }
+
+    /// The ledger of the store rooted at `directory`, rebuilt from what the store itself holds.
+    ///
+    /// Two sources, in this order, and neither of them is a file this module owns:
+    ///
+    /// 1. **the log**, replayed read-only ([`replay::recover`]): every payload the write path
+    ///    accepted, which is the store's whole history of *names* even where retention has since
+    ///    expired the segments that carried them;
+    /// 2. **the sidecar indexes of the segments the store still holds**
+    ///    ([`compaction::live_indexes`]): the prune, which is what drops the names the store has
+    ///    forgotten and refolds every survivor's window to the extremes the indexes state.
+    ///
+    /// # Errors
+    ///
+    /// [`Error::Store`] when the log, the manifest or an index cannot be read, and
+    /// [`Error::Protocol`] when the log or the manifest is not this build's.
